  "title": "I want a dependency-free JavaScript implementation of RFC 8785, the JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS), that I can drop into our receipt-signing code: one ES module exporting canonicalize(value) whose output is byte-exact for every input and output pair in the published cyberphone json-canonicalization test vectors, including the ECMAScript number-serialization edge cases, and that refuses what RFC 8785 forbids, such as non-finite numbers and lone surrogates.",
  "brief": "The requester's words: \"I want a dependency-free JavaScript implementation of RFC 8785, the JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS), that I can drop into our receipt-signing code: one ES module exporting canonicalize(value) whose output is byte-exact for every input and output pair in the published cyberphone json-canonicalization test vectors, including the ECMAScript number-serialization edge cases, and that refuses what RFC 8785 forbids, such as non-finite numbers and lone surrogates.\"\n\nBuild that module, `jcs.mjs`. These are the questions a maintainer of a JCS implementation would check first:\n\n- The published vectors are the six input/output pairs in cyberphone's `testdata` folder and all 100,000,000 lines of `es6testfile100m.txt`. The number lines open with the RFC 8785 Appendix B samples: minus zero, the smallest subnormal, the largest double, 2^53, the switch between plain and exponent notation at 1e21 and at 1e-7, and a round-half-even case. Every line must come out byte for byte.\n- Property names sort by UTF-16 code units (RFC 8785 section 3.2.3), so the key U+1F600 (stored as the code units D83D DE00) sorts before U+FB33. Sorting by code point or by UTF-8 bytes puts those two keys the other way round.\n- Strings keep their Unicode exactly as given, with no normalization (section 3.1), and escape only what section 3.2.2.2 lists. U+2028, U+2029, DEL and U+0080 stay raw.\n- Refusing means canonicalize throws and returns nothing. That covers NaN, Infinity and -Infinity anywhere in the value (section 3.2.2.3), and any string or property name that is not well-formed UTF-16: a lone high surrogate, a lone low surrogate or a reversed pair (section 3.2.2.2). The cyberphone Go implementation was fixed on 2026-10-11 because it wrote a reversed pair as U+FFFD instead of refusing it.\n- The requester has not said what canonicalize does with a JavaScript value that has no JSON form: undefined, a function, a symbol, a BigInt, a cycle, a Map, or an object with toJSON such as a Date. JSON.stringify drops some of these and converts others. Choose the rule, state it at the top of the module, and record why.\n- The requester has not said where the receipt-signing code runs. The check runs Node 20 or later. A module that uses only ECMAScript built-ins also runs in browsers, workers, Deno and Bun.\n- The requester has not said what licence the module ships under. Code adapted from an existing implementation keeps that implementation's licence terms, so record where any adapted code came from.",
